{"parentUuid":"2e77ed09-e2e4-4dca-80f5-9245097c8e1e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aa43418fbebf671c6","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CffjCUesUJCtG76itSyG1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The tenth sr-checks run left two refusals, and I fixed both and started an eleventh run, which is still going. Nothing is pushed yet, so there is no PR or merge.\n\n- **ADR trimmed.** The `adr-grounded` judge said the quote \"set a low token budget to reprodcue then\" only covers the low-token-budget decision. I cut `adr/mock-compaction-trigger/ADR.md` to that one rule: a mock compacts when the run sets a low token budget (Codex's `model_auto_compact_token_limit`) and its estimate passes it. I removed the claude-mock rule, the 4-chars-per-token figure, where the budget lives and the deviation requirement. The commit carries the same quote.\n- **Cursor evidence.** The `capability-grounded` judge said my first cursor run, a bare `/summarize`, applied no context pressure and so couldn't show absence. I recorded a second attempt in `cursor-mock/snapshots/runs/compaction-transcript-continuity` with over 100k tokens of tool output. No `preCompact` fired and the transcript has only plain user and assistant lines. I dropped the old `/summarize` sample, because its prompt no longer matched the setup, and rewrote the cell's reason to match the new run.\n\nThe cursor evidence is still weak. About 100k tokens did not reach cursor's threshold, and I could not find a way to set one headless, so the run does not prove cursor lacks compaction. If the judge refuses again, the options are a much larger recording (costly) or leaving cursor unresolved. Tell me which you prefer.\n\nIf this run is clean, I push, open the PR, watch CI and admin-merge once every check passes."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1681,"cache_read_input_tokens":2